From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 09:41:54 -0500
Subject: feat: settings and the extension list in sync (ADR 0037)

Sidebar sync can now carry two more parts, each with a box of its own in
sync's settings and on the join sheet: Settings and Extensions. Both boxes
are on for a new join. A computer that already synced before this version
gets neither part until the person ticks its box, so no setting changes
under anyone after an update.

Settings: one table in the code lists every Stedding preference as synced
or staying, with the reason for each one that stays (Mac-only and
Windows-only settings, sync's own name, place and sign-in, sizes measured
against the screen, shortcuts and Stedding's internal state). A test fails
when a preference is in neither list. Each synced setting keeps its later
edit on the sidebar's clock. Edits are heard through preference observers;
a value applied from another computer is not stamped as an edit here. The
stamps are kept in the ledger, so they survive a restart. Routes travel by
the synced Space they name, under the shared id the join's pairs gave it,
and land on the paired Space here; a route to a Space that is not synced
stays on its computer and no other computer removes it. The first join
where both computers have changed settings asks whose to start from; a
fresh install, every setting still its default, takes the other's.

Extensions: each computer lists its Chrome Web Store extensions by id and
name. Another computer offers the ones it lacks in sync's settings, with an
Install button each, and once in a bar for each set of new offers. Install
goes only through Chromium's own Web Store confirmation; an offer declined
there is not offered again from that computer, and an extension uninstalled
here is not offered here again. Nothing is ever removed or turned off
because of another computer, and no extension data travels.

Both parts are new keys of the file's sync block, which an older Stedding
never reads: it ignores them and its sidebar still syncs.

Why: GitHub issue 109; the owner chose ADR 0037 (2026-11-15) for 0.6.0 (PLAN decision 23). Spec: sidebar-file Sf36, Sf58-Sf69.
Removable when: never; part of Stedding's own sync.
